<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <SkyscannerWidget origin="London" originCode="LHR" destination="Toronto" destinationCode="YYZ" />
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Airlines Flying Nonstop from London to Toronto</h2>
      <p>The London to Toronto corridor is well-served by both legacy and leisure carriers, giving travellers genuine options across economy, premium economy, and business class.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Air Canada</strong> — Multiple daily nonstop flights from LHR to YYZ with Signature Class business cabin and a strong Aeroplan redemption program.</li>
        <li><strong>British Airways</strong> — Daily nonstop from LHR to YYZ with Club World business class and competitive Avios redemption opportunities.</li>
        <li><strong>WestJet</strong> — Seasonal and year-round nonstop service from LHR to YYZ offering competitive economy and premium fares.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Westbound vs Eastbound Flight Times</h2>
      <p>Flying westbound from London to Toronto takes approximately 8.5 hours due to headwinds from the jet stream. The eastbound return from Toronto to London is typically 7 to 7.5 hours. Overnight westbound departures from Heathrow are common and deliver you to Toronto in the afternoon local time.</p>
      <div class="tip-box"><p>An evening departure from Heathrow arriving mid-afternoon in Toronto is the most practical routing — you clear customs with energy to spare and get your first Canadian day.</p></div>
      <h2>Tips for London to Toronto Nonstop</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Air Canada Aeroplan</strong> miles are among the best ways to book business class on this route — watch for partner award availability on British Airways metal as well.</li>
        <li><strong>Premium Economy</strong> is a strong value proposition on the 8.5-hour westbound leg — significantly more comfortable than economy for a modest upcharge.</li>
        <li><strong>Book in advance</strong> — this route is heavily used by both business and leisure travellers and fares spike around Canadian holidays and UK school breaks.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
